{"id":171,"date":"2026-07-09T14:30:49","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:30:49","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/opening-the-treasure-lake-place-a-camp-owners-season-checklist\/"},"modified":"2026-07-15T21:04:07","modified_gmt":"2026-07-15T21:04:07","slug":"opening-the-treasure-lake-place-a-camp-owners-season-checklist","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/opening-the-treasure-lake-place-a-camp-owners-season-checklist\/","title":{"rendered":"That Third Addition Broke Your DuBois Home&#8217;s Airflow Math"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-cover alignfull s-hero-cover\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:2rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:2rem\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-contrast-background-color has-background-dim-70 has-background-dim\"><\/span><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"800\" height=\"600\" class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background wp-image-202\" alt=\"mini split heat pump\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg 800w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px\" \/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-cover-is-layout-ff0c90b8 wp-block-cover-is-layout-constrained\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center has-base-color has-text-color s-hero-cover__title wp-block-heading\">Your additions need their own climate<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center s-lead has-base-color has-text-color has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">DuBois lumber-era homes were built small and expanded haphazardly\u2014now they&#039;re full of disconnected HVAC zones that one thermostat can&#039;t manage.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Schedule the Walkthrough<\/a><\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline is-style-outline--1\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"#content\">Learn More<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why DuBois homes feel uncomfortable<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">DuBois grew during Pennsylvania&#039;s lumber boom, and its housing stock reflects the working-class roots of that era: modest cottages and two-story boxes built quickly and cheaply for sawmill and railroad workers. Over the decades, many of these homes gained additions\u2014sunrooms, enclosed porches, garage conversions, second-story expansions\u2014creating floor plans that sprawl in unpredictable directions.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The original homes were small enough that a single coal stove or gravity furnace in the basement could heat the whole structure. The additions were never integrated into the original heating plan, leaving them with baseboard heaters, through-wall units, or nothing at all.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The result: a main floor that&#039;s reasonably comfortable, a bedroom addition that&#039;s always cold, a finished basement that roasts when the upstairs is set to a comfortable temperature.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Tacked-on additions break airflow balance<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Walk through a typical DuBois home on West Long Avenue or along Brady Street, and you&#039;ll see the history of expansions: mismatched flooring where an addition meets the original structure, ceiling height changes, doors that lead to spaces that feel like afterthoughts. These additions were often built without permits or professional design, framed by homeowners or handymen who prioritized speed and cost over integration. The core issue is thermal isolation.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Additions often have different insulation levels, ceiling heights, and exposure than the original home, creating heat loss or gain rates that don&#039;t match the rest of the house. A sunroom addition with single-pane windows and minimal insulation loses heat faster than the main house, so when the thermostat is satisfied, the sunroom is still cold.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Extending a duct from the existing trunk line doesn&#039;t solve it\u2014the new duct is undersized, the airflow is weak, and the furnace isn&#039;t big enough to condition the extra square footage. The fix requires treating each addition as a separate zone with its own heating and cooling needs.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">That might mean installing a ductless mini-split head in the sunroom, running dedicated duct lines from the furnace with a zone damper, or adding a separate air handler that serves only the addition.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Zone dampers direct airflow independently to problem areas<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Mini-splits heat and cool isolated additions without ductwork<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Proper return air design prevents pressure imbalances<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Load calculations ensure equipment matches actual demand<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b.jpg\" alt=\"attic insulation\" class=\"wp-image-190\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Mice holes in attic insulation&quot; by jaredhouliston, CC0, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How zone control systems actually work<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Zone control systems use motorized dampers installed in ductwork to direct airflow where it&#039;s needed, allowing different areas of the home to be heated or cooled independently. A home with three zones\u2014original main floor, second-floor addition, and finished basement\u2014can have separate thermostats for each, and the system modulates dampers to deliver conditioned air only to the zones calling for heating or cooling.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The key is ensuring the furnace or air handler can operate safely with some dampers closed. When a zone damper shuts, static pressure in the system increases because the blower is still moving air but has fewer outlets to deliver it through.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Modern zone systems include bypass dampers or variable-speed blowers that modulate to maintain safe pressure, but retrofitting zoning onto an older single-speed system without these safeguards can cause more problems than it solves. Each zone needs adequate return airflow to maintain proper circulation. When a zone damper opens to supply conditioned air, that air has to get back to the furnace to be reconditioned.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In homes where additions were built without return ducts, the air gets trapped, creating positive pressure that forces conditioned air out through leaks and gaps. The solution is adding returns in each zone, which often requires cutting through walls or floors to create pathways back to the central return plenum.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large alignwide\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"thermostat - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1479\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-thermostat-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Solutions for disconnected spaces<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-feature-row is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg\" alt=\"hvac technician repair\" class=\"wp-image-188\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;AC Service&quot; by SuperTech HVAC Services, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Mini-split systems<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Wall-mounted units serve unreachable additions independently. No ductwork needed, independent thermostats, efficient heating and cooling. Ideal for sunrooms, converted garages, finished attics.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1475\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Whole-house zoning<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Motorized dampers in existing ductwork create separate zones with independent control. Works best when furnace has spare capacity and return air can be balanced. Requires variable-speed equipment.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b.jpg\" alt=\"attic insulation\" class=\"wp-image-189\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;The R11 attic insulation&quot; by zieak, BY, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Duct redesign<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Replacing undersized or poorly routed ductwork restores balanced airflow. Sealing leaks recovers lost capacity. Often paired with zone dampers or new registers for targeted delivery.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"ductwork - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1478\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Supplemental air handlers<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Small dedicated units serve specific additions on their own trunk line. Gives the main furnace breathing room and ensures the addition gets adequate conditioned air year-round.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1.png\" alt=\"ductwork - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1759\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Airflow balancing<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Adjusting dampers and register sizes redistributes existing air to under-served rooms. Flow hood measurements confirm each room gets the right volume. Quick win when capacity is adequate.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1023\" height=\"575\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9060093881_9c8364f29c_b.jpg\" alt=\"mini split heat pump\" class=\"wp-image-201\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9060093881_9c8364f29c_b.jpg 1023w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9060093881_9c8364f29c_b-300x169.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9060093881_9c8364f29c_b-768x432.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 1023px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Back of house after Lennox Mini-split heat pump\/AC was installed into the Great Room.&quot; by Lani. Mar 13 1941 to Dec 13 2018. Rest in Peace, BY-ND, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Heat pump additions<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Modern variable-speed heat pumps reduce temperature swings and adapt to part-load conditions. When paired with zone control, they&#039;re efficient across the full heating season in DuBois winters.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">DuBois-specific heating challenges<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Clearfield County averages 6,800 heating degree days, requiring equipment sized for sustained cold output. Homes on ridgelines near I-80 face wind exposure that increases heat loss by 15\u201320% compared to valley locations. Builder-grade equipment installed in 1990s subdivisions was often undersized for actual winter loads, causing utility bills to climb and equipment to run constantly. Variable-speed furnaces reduce temperature swings from 4\u00b0F down to under 1\u00b0F\u2014a measurable comfort gain that homeowners notice immediately.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills s-pills--glass\">\n<li>6,800 heating degree days annually<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Multi-addition homes common pre-1970<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Ductless systems avoid wall demolition<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Zone control enables independent comfort<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Clearfield County wind exposure: +15% capacity needed<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Variable-speed units match DuBois winters<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How we diagnose your zone problem<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>Inspect the ductwork \u2014 Visual survey of trunk lines, branches, and connections. Identify disconnects, leaks, undersized runs, and routing that misses problem areas. Use duct imaging to spot obstructions.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Calculate actual loads \u2014 Manual J calculation for each room based on insulation, window area, orientation, and exposure. Reveals which spaces are thermal problem children and why the furnace is struggling.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Measure airflow distribution \u2014 Flow hood readings at each register show exactly how much conditioned air reaches each room. Identifies over-served and under-served zones and confirms duct sizing adequacy.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Assess equipment capacity \u2014 Furnace and AC specifications versus actual home load. Determine if oversizing is needed, if the existing equipment can be salvaged, or if replacement unlocks better zone options.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Design the solution \u2014 Recommend zoning, mini-splits, duct upgrades, or hybrid approach based on cost, disruption, and long-term comfort goals. Provide itemized scoping so you understand what&#039;s required.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">DuBois HVAC zoning questions<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I just add a duct to my cold addition?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Only if the existing furnace has spare capacity and the ductwork can be extended without creating airflow imbalances. Most of the time, simply tapping into an existing trunk line and running a duct to the addition results in weak airflow and robs air from other rooms. A proper solution sizes the duct correctly, ensures the furnace can handle the added load, and balances the entire system afterward.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>How much does a ductless mini-split cost for one room?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A single-zone mini-split system typically runs $3,000 to $5,000 installed, depending on the unit&#039;s capacity, the brand, and installation complexity. Multi-zone systems cost more but provide better value per room when you&#039;re conditioning several additions. Factor in electrical work if your panel needs upgrading to handle the additional load.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Will a zone control system work with my old furnace?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Depends on the furnace. Single-speed furnaces can be retrofitted with zoning, but they need bypass dampers or zoning-compatible controls to avoid overpressure issues. Variable-speed furnaces handle zoning better because they modulate airflow automatically. If your furnace is near end of life, consider replacing it with a variable-speed model that&#039;s designed to work with zone systems.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why does the room farthest from my furnace always feel cold?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Duct sizing or balancing issue. Air takes the path of least resistance, so it fills the main trunk line and short-circuits to the nearest registers. Distant rooms get whatever&#039;s left. Solutions include adding larger ducts to distant zones, installing balancing dampers to restrict flow to over-served areas, and ensuring adequate return air so the system isn&#039;t starved.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I control zones with just a smart thermostat?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No. A thermostat controls the furnace on\/off, but it can&#039;t direct air to different rooms. You need motorized dampers installed in the ductwork to actually split the zones. A smart thermostat can trigger zone dampers if they&#039;re wired to respond, but the dampers themselves do the work.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Performance check for your system<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Temperature swing between cycles exceeds 3\u00b0F \u2014 staging or fan speed issue<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Blower runs but airflow feels weak \u2014 filter restricted or duct static too high<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Rooms farthest from furnace always lag \u2014 duct sizing or balancing inadequate<\/li>\n\n\n<li>System runs fine in fall but struggles in January \u2014 capacity undersized for design temperature<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Utility bill climbs year over year with same usage \u2014 equipment efficiency degrading<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--warm-gradient has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-cta has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Start with a performance evaluation<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We measure airflow, calculate loads, and identify exactly which solution fits your home&#039;s layout. No guessing, no upsells\u2014just a clear report and a price for the fix you actually need.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Schedule the Assessment<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity s-sep s-sep--dot\"